
+ \qquad F(p(0))=\alpha(0).
+\end{equation}
+Then
+\[
+ \frac{\dd}{\dd t}F(p(t))=\alpha'(t),
+\]
+so $F(p(t))=\alpha(t)$ while the solution exists.  By
+\eqref{eq:malgrange-uniform},
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:linear-growth}
+ \|p'(t)\|\le C_\alpha(1+\rho(p(t)))
+\end{equation}
+for a constant depending only on the path.  Since
+$|\rho'(t)|\le\|p'(t)\|$, Gronwall's inequality bounds $\rho(p(t))$ on
+every finite time interval.  The sublevel sets of $\rho$ are compact in
+$\mathcal T_d$, so the ordinary ODE continuation theorem extends the
+solution across all of $[0,1]$.  This excludes both ordinary Euclidean
+escape and approach to a deleted coordinate hyperplane.
+
+Transport along the reversed path gives the inverse map, and smooth
+dependence on initial conditions gives a diffeomorphism of fibers.
+Applying the same construction to the straight radial paths in a small
+disc compactly contained in $U$ yields a smooth local trivialization.
+The homology groups therefore form a local system.
+\end{proof}
 
